 id:"ENG008-BM-E08",title:"Why Inflation Expectations Can Affect Inflation Itself",genre:"economy",
 text:`Inflation is usually described as a change in prices that has already happened. Yet expectations about future inflation can also influence present economic behaviour. If households and businesses believe prices will rise rapidly, they may change wages, contracts, purchases and investment before the expected increase fully appears.

Workers who expect living costs to rise may seek larger wage increases. Businesses facing higher wage bills may raise prices to protect margins. Suppliers may write shorter contracts or include clauses that adjust prices automatically. In this way, expectations can become part of the process through which inflation persists.

This does not mean expectations alone create every inflation episode. A sharp rise in energy prices, a crop failure, supply-chain disruption or currency depreciation can increase costs regardless of what people previously believed. The important point is that expectations can influence how an initial shock spreads and how long it lasts.

Credibility therefore matters for central banks. If people believe a central bank will eventually restore price stability, temporary shocks may have less influence on long-term wage and price setting. If that credibility is weak, businesses may assume high inflation will continue and behave accordingly.

Measuring expectations is difficult. Surveys ask households, firms or professional forecasters what inflation they anticipate, while financial markets provide measures derived from asset prices. These indicators do not always agree. Households may focus heavily on frequently purchased items such as food and fuel, while market measures can reflect liquidity and risk premiums as well as pure inflation expectations.

Time horizon matters too. Expectations for next year can rise after an energy shock while five-year expectations remain stable. Treating all horizons as one number can hide whether people view inflation as temporary or persistent.

Policy communication tries to influence this distinction. A central bank may explain why it expects a shock to fade, but communication works only if the explanation is credible and consistent with later actions.

There is also a risk of overreacting to expectations data. A short-lived survey jump does not necessarily mean a wage-price spiral has begun. Analysts need to compare expectations with wage growth, pricing behaviour, labour-market conditions and the underlying source of inflation.

Expectations can also vary across groups. Renters, homeowners, firms and retirees face different price baskets and may form views from different experiences. An average survey response can therefore conceal important variation.

For policymakers, the challenge is not simply to “manage expectations” through words. Expectations respond to actual policy, recent inflation and people's confidence that institutions will act as promised.

The broader lesson is that inflation is partly forward-looking. Today's decisions can reflect beliefs about tomorrow, and those decisions can in turn affect tomorrow's prices. Understanding inflation therefore requires attention both to current cost pressures and to the expectations that shape how households and firms respond to them.`,
