 id:"ENG008-BM-H06",title:"Why Average Treatment Effects Can Hide Patient Differences",genre:"health",
 text:`Clinical trials often report an average treatment effect: the difference in outcomes between a treatment group and a comparison group. The average is essential because it summarises evidence across many participants. Yet it can hide important variation in who benefits, who does not and who experiences harm.

Suppose a medicine lowers blood pressure by an average of six units. That result does not mean every participant's pressure falls by six. Some may respond strongly, some slightly and some not at all. A small number may experience side effects that make the treatment unsuitable despite the average benefit.

Researchers therefore examine subgroups, but subgroup analysis is difficult. If a trial tests many age groups, genetic markers and baseline conditions, some apparently large differences may occur by chance. Small subgroups also produce imprecise estimates.

Pre-specification helps. If researchers identify an expected interaction before analysing the data, the result is generally more credible than a pattern discovered after trying dozens of comparisons. Exploratory findings can still be valuable, but they usually need confirmation in later studies.

Biological plausibility matters as well. A subgroup difference supported by a known mechanism may deserve more attention than an isolated statistical result with no clear explanation.

Another issue is absolute versus relative benefit. A drug that reduces risk by 20 per cent has different practical value for someone whose starting risk is 20 per cent than for someone whose starting risk is 1 per cent. The relative effect may be similar while the number of events prevented is very different.

Patient preferences also matter. Two people with the same expected medical benefit may value side effects differently because of work, family responsibilities or tolerance for uncertainty.

Personalised medicine aims to use this variation, but prediction is harder than explanation. Knowing that a subgroup performed better in a trial does not automatically mean a model can accurately predict an individual patient's response.

External validity creates another challenge. Trial participants may be healthier, younger or more closely monitored than patients seen in routine practice. A subgroup result must therefore be interpreted in the population where treatment will actually be used.

Regulators and clinicians balance these layers of evidence. The average effect establishes whether a treatment works overall, while subgroup evidence may guide more targeted use when it is sufficiently reliable.

The danger lies at both extremes. Ignoring variation can treat the average as if it describes every patient. Overemphasising weak subgroup findings can create false precision and fragment evidence into unreliable categories.

Good interpretation asks how strong the overall effect is, whether subgroup differences were anticipated, how precise they are, whether they make biological sense and whether they matter in absolute terms.

The broader lesson is that averages are not mistakes; they are summaries. Their limitation is that summaries compress variation. Clinical decisions become stronger when the average result is combined with credible evidence about baseline risk, patient characteristics and preferences without pretending that every individual response can be predicted exactly.`,
