 id:"ENG008-BM-EN05",title:"Why Recycling Rates Can Hide Material Loss",genre:"environment",
 text:`Cities often report recycling rates as the share of collected waste sent into a recycling stream. The number is useful, but it can create an overly optimistic picture if it is interpreted as the share of material that ultimately becomes a new product.

A household may correctly place packaging in a recycling bin, yet the material can still be lost later. Food contamination may make paper unusable. Mixed plastics may be difficult to separate. Glass can break into fragments that are uneconomic to recover. Some collected material is rejected at sorting facilities.

This distinction creates two important measures: collection and recovery. Collection asks how much material enters the recycling system. Recovery asks how much usable secondary material leaves it.

Export complicates the picture. A city may count material as recycled when it is shipped to another region for processing. If the receiving facility rejects part of the load, the final outcome may differ from the original local statistic.

Quality matters as well as quantity. Recycled paper fibres degrade with repeated use, and some plastics can be recycled only into lower-value products. A tonne of material collected does not always replace a tonne of virgin material.

This does not make household sorting irrelevant. Cleaner inputs can reduce contamination and improve recovery. Clearer packaging labels and simpler material design can also make sorting easier.

Producers have a role because packaging design determines whether materials can be economically separated. A technically recyclable product may rarely be recycled in practice if facilities cannot process it at scale.

For policymakers, focusing only on the household bin can therefore miss upstream and downstream causes. Deposit systems, producer responsibility, sorting technology and product design can affect the final recovery rate.

Measurement should follow material through the system. Some programmes now report both the quantity collected and the quantity rejected after sorting. Others estimate how much secondary material actually re-enters manufacturing.

There are trade-offs in measurement complexity. Tracking every tonne to a final product can be expensive, especially when material moves through several companies or countries. A practical system may therefore use audited facility data and sampling rather than perfect traceability.

The same caution applies to claims about “100 per cent recyclable” packaging. The phrase may describe technical possibility under ideal conditions, not the real probability that an item placed in a local bin will become a new product.

Consumer communication should distinguish recyclability from actual recycling. One concerns whether a material can be processed; the other concerns whether collection, sorting, markets and facilities actually make that process happen.

The broader lesson is that recycling performance is a chain. Success at one stage does not guarantee success at the next. A strong policy therefore measures not only what enters the system but also what survives sorting and returns to productive use.`,
